## Goal
Visualize the relationship between the six samples using principal component analysis (PCA) on gene-level counts. The analysis will show how untreated and treated samples separate in the space of the most variable genes, enabling assessment of biological replicate consistency and condition-level separation.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |

## Method
1. Load gene counts from the resolved counts_gene artifact using pandas in Python (gars-bio environment).
2. Filter to genes with at least 10 counts across all samples to remove low-abundance noise.
3. Apply log2(counts + 1) transformation for variance stabilization.
4. Perform PCA using scikit-learn's PCA, retaining all components. Calculate the percentage of variance explained by each principal component.
5. Generate a biplot showing PC1 vs. PC2 with sample points colored by condition (untreated: blue, treated: red). Label each point with its sample ID.
6. Create a scree plot showing cumulative variance explained (PC1–PC4).
7. Generate the plots using matplotlib, saved to results/ directory.
8. Write a summary table with per-sample PC1 and PC2 coordinates and their condition labels.
9. Save Python script used to results/pca_script.py for reproducibility.

